# Target C-II — the near-full exact-force shore contains one linear paired destination regime

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** the no-positive-density-TB1 branch of the selected one-sided high-`t` shore through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** near-full-shore growing force diagonal; gap-free bilateral force weave; same-host diameter exclusion; full fixed-birth-side nesting; exact diameter-normal order; canonical C67 direction separation; full-source midpoint localization  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_near_full_paired_destination_trichotomy_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The near-full-shore diagonal gives exact-force centers at asymptotic density

\[
{c\over2},
\qquad
c={113\over5{,}000{,}000},
\qquad
{c\over2}={113\over10{,}000{,}000}=1.13\times10^{-5}.
\tag{NP.1}
\]

Its force-pair projective gap is permitted to tend to zero. The gap-free bilateral theorem removes the only reason that the previous paired-destination normal form had to start from a smaller fixed-angle population. After at most six deletions, **every** center in the near-full diagonal has one force bar born on each physical host arc, with a macroscopic birth rung and partner rung. The two birth-side lanes can therefore be nested simultaneously on essentially the whole one-sided shore.

The result is a near-full-density paired normal form: after ordering the centers toward the common hub, the destination pattern changes at most twice,

```text
TT  ->  mixed  ->  OO,
```

and one consecutive regime has asymptotic density at least

\[
\boxed{{c\over6}={113\over30{,}000{,}000}=3.766666666\ldots\times10^{-6}.}
\tag{NP.2}
\]

All growing-depth force/KKT/train-grid metadata survives on that regime.

---

## 1. Start from the near-full growing exact-force diagonal

Use the no-positive-density-TB1 diagonal sequence from the near-full-shore theorem. There are source diameters `D_k -> infinity`, radii `r_k -> infinity`, and center sets `Q_k` such that

\[
\boxed{
|Q_k|>\left({c\over2}-o(1)\right)D_k.
}
\tag{NP.3}
\]

Every center lies on one strict side of the common hub. Its full radius-`r_k` neighborhood is exact-antipodal, every internal interface carries an exact positive two-bar force pair, all rows in the block have one exact oriented phase, and the normalized local switching/KKT complexity tends to zero. Every force bar retains the host transverse margin

\[
|d\cdot Jv|\ge c_H-o(1),
\qquad
c_H=0.453819885927\ldots .
\tag{NP.4}
\]

The pair-gap parameter `rho_k` may tend to zero.

Apply the gap-free bilateral force theorem to the exact force pair at every center. Delete the at most six unilateral centers. This finite loss is absorbed into `o(D_k)`. Hence at every remaining center `i` there are bars

\[
h_i^A=x_i^Ay_i^A,
\qquad
h_i^B=x_i^By_i^B,
\tag{NP.5}
\]

with

\[
x_i^A\text{ on physical host }A,
\qquad
x_i^B\text{ on physical host }B,
\tag{NP.6}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
|x_i^A-x_i^B|,
|y_i^A-y_i^B|
\ge(c_H-o(1))D_k.
}
\tag{NP.7}
\]

The exact force identity is retained:

\[
G_i=D_k(\lambda_i^A d_i^A+\lambda_i^B d_i^B),
\qquad \lambda_i^A,\lambda_i^B>0.
\tag{NP.8}
\]

On every growing exact-phase neighborhood, the two transverse-sign lanes form the complete bipartite crossing weave from the gap-free theorem. Across the whole center population, one of the two ordinary reaction-axis lifts contains at least half the centers and has the same cross-sign crossing property. No endpoint-degree thinning is used for either statement.

---

## 2. Both birth-side lanes are strictly nested on the near-full population

Write the retained center indices in increasing order toward the hub:

\[
i_1<i_2<\cdots<i_N<k,
\qquad
N>\left({c\over2}-o(1)\right)D.
\tag{NP.9}
\]

For the bar born on `A`, write the hubward endpoint region as `R_{b_s^A}`; similarly write `R_{b_s^B}` for the `B`-born bar. Exact selected-cut supports are

\[
I_s^A=\{i_s+1,\ldots,b_s^A\},
\qquad
I_s^B=\{i_s+1,\ldots,b_s^B\}.
\tag{NP.10}
\]

Same-host diameter exclusion gives

\[
y_s^A\in Q^B\cup T_m,
\qquad
y_s^B\in Q^A\cup T_m,
\tag{NP.11}
\]

where `Q^A,Q^B` are the hubward portions of the two physical hosts and `T_m` is the far terminal boundary piece.

Apply the fixed-birth-host nesting theorem to the complete `A` lane. The endpoint order on the contiguous destination segment `Q^B\cup T_m` gives

\[
\boxed{b_1^A\ge b_2^A\ge\cdots\ge b_N^A.}
\tag{NP.12}
\]

Apply it independently to the complete `B` lane:

\[
\boxed{b_1^B\ge b_2^B\ge\cdots\ge b_N^B.}
\tag{NP.13}
\]

Because the birth indices increase strictly, both support lanes are strictly nested:

\[
\boxed{
I_1^A\supsetneq I_2^A\supsetneq\cdots\supsetneq I_N^A,
}
\tag{NP.14}
\]

\[
\boxed{
I_1^B\supsetneq I_2^B\supsetneq\cdots\supsetneq I_N^B.
}
\tag{NP.15}
\]

No density factor is paid. This is the old paired nesting theorem applied at the stronger near-full diagonal scale made available by gap-free bilateral birth.

---

## 3. The paired destination word has only three consecutive regimes

Use the established boundary-piece convention in which the terminal piece has maximal path-region tag `m`, while the opposite-host segment continues with decreasing labels `m-1,m-2,...,k`. Thus

\[
y_s^\sigma\in T_m
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
b_s^\sigma=m,
\qquad \sigma\in\{A,B\}.
\tag{NP.16}
\]

Since each `b_s^sigma` sequence is nonincreasing, terminal membership is a prefix in each lane. There are cutoffs

\[
r_A,r_B\in\{0,1,\ldots,N\}
\tag{NP.17}
\]

such that

\[
y_s^A\in T_m\iff s\le r_A,
\qquad
y_s^B\in T_m\iff s\le r_B.
\tag{NP.18}
\]

Put

\[
r_-:=\min(r_A,r_B),
\qquad
r_+:=\max(r_A,r_B).
\]

The full center population then consists of exactly three consecutive regimes, any of which may be empty:

1. `1<=s<=r_-`: **TT**, both force bars terminate in `T_m`;
2. `r_-<s<=r_+`: **mixed**, exactly one force bar terminates in `T_m` and the other goes to its opposite physical host;
3. `r_+<s<=N`: **OO**, both force bars go to their opposite physical hosts.

Their cardinalities sum to `N`. Hence

\[
\boxed{
\max\{M_{TT},M_{mix},M_{OO}\}\ge {N\over3}.
}
\tag{NP.19}
\]

Together with (NP.9), one consecutive paired regime satisfies

\[
\boxed{
M_*>
\left({c\over6}-o(1)\right)D
=
\left({113\over30{,}000{,}000}-o(1)\right)D.
}
\tag{NP.20}
\]

Numerically the leading coefficient is

\[
\boxed{3.76666666667\times10^{-6}.}
\tag{NP.21}
\]

The previous paired-destination theorem started from the older deep-force density ceiling `c_0=c/4` and approached `c_0/3=c/12`. The new floor `c/6` is exactly twice that asymptotic coefficient.

---

## 4. The TT regime forces twice the terminal direction density

Suppose TT is the large regime and let

\[
M:=M_{TT}>
\left({c\over6}-o(1)\right)D.
\tag{NP.22}
\]

There are two force bars per center, and switching sets at different interfaces are edge-disjoint. Section 1 also gives four distinct endpoints within each pair. Thus TT contains `2M` distinct actual diameter edges whose hubward endpoints all lie in `T_m`.

Canonical C67 separates distinct projective diameter directions by

\[
\delta_D=2\arcsin{1\over2D}\ge {1\over D}.
\tag{NP.23}
\]

Let

\[
L_D=O(D^{3/4}),
\qquad
\epsilon_D=
\arcsin\!\left({L_D\over D/2-L_D}\right)=o(1)
\tag{NP.24}
\]

be the common midpoint/radial-direction error. The same projective-circle packing argument used in the existing terminal-span theorem gives

\[
\operatorname{angdiam}_o(T_m\cap S)
\ge
\min\left\{{\pi\over3},(2M-1)\delta_D\right\}
-2\epsilon_D.
\tag{NP.25}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\operatorname{angdiam}_o(T_m\cap S)
\ge {c\over3}
={113\over15{,}000{,}000}
=7.533333333\ldots\times10^{-6}.
}
\tag{NP.26}
\]

The `pi/3` cap is inactive at this density. Radial projection to radius `D/2` also yields

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\operatorname{diam}(T_m\cap S)\over D}
\ge
\sin\!\left({c\over6}\right)
=3.76666666665\ldots\times10^{-6}.
}
\tag{NP.27}
\]

The terminal branch therefore carries a macroscopic two-lane terminal object at the full near-shore density scale, without requiring a fixed projective gap inside each force pair.

---

## 5. What the other two regimes retain

If **OO** is the large regime, every center supplies two simultaneous opposite-host diameter bars:

\[
A\to B,
\qquad
B\to A.
\]

Each birth-side lane is strictly nested by (NP.14)--(NP.15), and every growing exact-phase neighborhood has completely crossing transverse-sign force lanes. Every paired birth rung and partner rung has Euclidean length at least `(c_H-o(1))D`.

If **mixed** is the large regime, its orientation is fixed throughout the whole consecutive block. For example, after possibly interchanging `A,B`, every center has

\[
A\to T_m,
\qquad
B\to A.
\]

Again both support lanes are nested, every growing exact-phase neighborhood carries the complete cross-sign weave, and the macroscopic rung bounds persist.

In all three regimes the centers come from the near-full growing force diagonal. Therefore the following metadata is retained simultaneously:

- exact-antipodal radius `r_D -> infinity` around every center;
- an exact positive force pair at every internal interface of those neighborhoods;
- fixed individual host transverse derivative margin `c_H`;
- normalized switching/KKT complexity tending to zero;
- common exact row phase inside each growing block;
- common dense near-equilateral train grid;
- same-axis strictly ordered strip reactions.

The regime extraction costs only the factor three in (NP.19), plus a finite bilateral deletion.

---

## 6. Frontier effect

On the no-positive-density-TB1 side, the near-full exact-force obstruction is no longer an unordered population of local dipoles. Along a subsequence it contains a consecutive linear block of one of three paired global geometries at density at least `c/6`:

```text
TT:    two nested force lanes into the far terminal piece;
mixed: one nested terminal lane + one nested opposite-host lane;
OO:    two simultaneous opposite-host nested force lanes.
```

All three have macroscopic birth/partner rungs and complete cross-sign crossing weaves on their growing exact-phase neighborhoods, while keeping the growing exact-phase/common-grid/KKT structure.

C-II remains open. The live no-TB1 target is now to rule out these three paired regimes against the common near-equilateral train grid and near-central diameter geometry, or to convert one into a same-cardinality strict sub-`D` competitor. The separate positive-density strong-TB1 branch also remains open.

**Disposition:** lifted the paired nested-destination trichotomy to the asymptotically full one-sided exact-force density, forcing a `c/6` linear TT/mixed/OO regime and doubling the previous asymptotic paired-regime coefficient. C-II and Level I remain open.
